# `for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) s += holder.list[i] * t`: the list is read off a field in the turn. The typed copy moves that read in front and asks the list there for its storage, while the turn is walked:
# behind the head, so the head could no longer be held against the lesser of the bound and the length: every read asked `i < length`, and what hangs on a position inside the list fell with it:
# the kind of storage asked once for turns laid out, the int32 behind an element.
# Where the counter is held by a relation with a WRITTEN bound, its greatest value is known: `99 < length` is asked once, in front of the typed copy, beside the questions on the storage; the length is held above it and the read lies inside by the types.
# In `open` the bound is no written number, so nothing is asked so. In `past` the list is shorter than the bound, and the closed copy runs.
# -- source: a-list-asked-in-front-of-a-typed-copy-is-asked-there-whether-the-written-bound-of-the-counter-lies-inside.ts
